@@ -271,6 +271,34 @@ hyprmoncfgd --profile desk        # always apply this specific profile
 hyprmoncfgd --quiet               # suppress log output
 ```
 
+## IPC-only prototype
+
+The local prototype adds `hyprmoncfgd --ipc-only`, off by default. It keeps
+the IPC server, writer lock, generated include and Omarchy watcher suppression
+while managed. The TUI, panel and CLI can still discover hardware, edit profiles,
+preview changes, commit and save, or revert. Preview expiry and shutdown still
+roll back unconfirmed transactions, including workspace placement.
+
+Startup, hotplug, lid, resume, polling, power and orientation events cannot apply
+layouts, move workspaces, wake outputs or run display recovery in this mode.
+Forced-profile, management and automatic-selection requests cannot bypass that
+restriction. Explicit previews still use the existing apply engine, including
+its lid/orientation adjustments and targeted wake verification.
+
+An unmatched, sleeping, modeless or entirely disconnected setup remains available
+for IPC queries and explicit edits without automatic rescue. An external policy
+owner must make displays usable. Existing generated output rules still load
+whenever Hyprland reloads them. Startup leaves both files untouched, including
+missing or misplaced includes. An explicit preview repairs the managed include
+as part of its transaction, with the existing rollback safeguards.
+
+Status reports `daemon.ipc_only: true` when this mode is active. Older or ordinary
+daemons omit this field; external coordinators requiring exclusive policy must
+refuse an absent or false value. This local patch does not add panel controls:
+automatic selection controls have no layout effect in this mode. Rotation settings
+can be recorded, but immediate rotation reconciliation is suppressed. The host
+coordinator separately owns service configuration and installation.
+
 ## Forcing a specific profile
 
 Use `--profile <name>` to bypass automatic matching entirely. The daemon applies this one profile every time, regardless of what's connected. This is useful when you know exactly which setup you're on and want to eliminate any chance of a wrong match.
